1
00:00:00,324 --> 00:00:03,554
All over the world, people
gather in special buildings.

2
00:00:03,574 --> 00:00:04,941
Churches. Mosques.

3
00:00:04,961 --> 00:00:06,341
Temples. Synagogues.

4
00:00:06,361 --> 00:00:07,719
Shrines. They pray.

5
00:00:07,739 --> 00:00:09,773
They sing. They light candles.

6
00:00:09,793 --> 00:00:15,273
About three in every four people alive
today say they belong to a religion.

7
00:00:15,293 --> 00:00:20,148
And every culture anthropologists have
described has some kind of ritual, some

8
00:00:20,168 --> 00:00:25,446
way of mourning the dead, and some idea
of a world beyond the one we can see.

9
00:00:25,466 --> 00:00:27,135
But where did it all begin?

10
00:00:27,155 --> 00:00:29,686
Now take time back fifty thousand years.

11
00:00:29,706 --> 00:00:32,306
A small group stands around
a hole in the ground.

12
00:00:32,326 --> 00:00:33,818
No temple. No holy book.

13
00:00:33,838 --> 00:00:38,937
No priest. And yet, they lay someone
they loved gently into the earth.

14
00:00:38,957 --> 00:00:41,575
They sprinkle red ochre over the body.

15
00:00:41,595 --> 00:00:44,958
And they place something
beside them, for the journey.

16
00:00:44,978 --> 00:00:47,983
Now your brain is
probably saying, Cavemen?

17
00:00:48,003 --> 00:00:51,508
They were too busy surviving
to wonder about anything.

18
00:00:51,528 --> 00:00:55,229
Gods and rituals came later,
with cities and kings.

19
00:00:55,249 --> 00:00:56,905
At least, that's what we assume.

20
00:00:56,925 --> 00:00:59,025
But the evidence says something else.

21
00:00:59,045 --> 00:01:03,900
A small child, laid to rest seventy eight
thousand years ago, its head probably

22
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:08,825
on a pillow. Paintings of beings
that are half human, half animal.

23
00:01:08,845 --> 00:01:13,837
And giant carved stone pillars, raised
by hunter-gatherers, as farming was only

24
00:01:13,857 --> 00:01:18,393
just beginning. We can't know
exactly what ancient people believed.

25
00:01:18,413 --> 00:01:20,523
But we can see what they did.

26
00:01:20,543 --> 00:01:23,703
To find the beginning, start
with our animal cousins.

27
00:01:23,723 --> 00:01:26,980
Elephants seem to be drawn to
the bones of their own kind.

28
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,019
In Kenya, researchers laid out skulls
and ivory in front of wild elephants.

29
00:01:32,039 --> 00:01:36,594
The elephants spent more time sniffing
and touching elephant skulls than the

30
00:01:36,614 --> 00:01:39,119
skulls of other large animals.

31
00:01:39,139 --> 00:01:41,994
And the ivory interested them most of all.

32
00:01:42,014 --> 00:01:44,045
Chimpanzees react to death too.

33
00:01:44,065 --> 00:01:49,017
In Guinea, two chimp mothers kept carrying
their dead babies, one for nineteen

34
00:01:49,037 --> 00:01:51,219
days, the other for sixty eight.

35
00:01:51,239 --> 00:01:54,191
They kept grooming them,
and shooing away the flies.

36
00:01:54,211 --> 00:01:59,689
In a Scottish safari park, an old chimpanzee
called Pansy died, surrounded by her

37
00:01:59,709 --> 00:02:03,542
group. They groomed her, and
checked her for signs of life.

38
00:02:03,562 --> 00:02:07,318
Her daughter Rosie stayed near
the body all through the night.

39
00:02:07,338 --> 00:02:11,443
And for five days afterwards, none of
the chimps would sleep on the platform

40
00:02:11,463 --> 00:02:15,388
where Pansy died. Chimps do
other strange things, too.

41
00:02:15,408 --> 00:02:20,075
Jane Goodall saw chimps put on
dramatic displays at waterfalls.

42
00:02:20,095 --> 00:02:24,665
Swaying, splashing, throwing
branches, for no obvious reason.

43
00:02:24,685 --> 00:02:29,252
Afterwards, a chimp might sit on a
rock and just watch the falling water.

44
00:02:29,272 --> 00:02:33,592
Goodall wondered if they were feeling
something like wonder, or awe.

45
00:02:33,612 --> 00:02:38,838
And in West Africa, hidden cameras caught
chimps hurling stones at certain trees,

46
00:02:38,858 --> 00:02:43,974
with a loud call, again and again,
until piles of stones built up.

47
00:02:43,994 --> 00:02:46,199
Nobody knows why they do it.

48
00:02:46,219 --> 00:02:51,150
The researchers noticed it looks a little
like the stone heaps that humans build.

49
00:02:51,170 --> 00:02:53,575
We can't read an animal's mind.

50
00:02:53,595 --> 00:02:57,997
But the roots of mourning, and
maybe of wonder, could be very old.

51
00:02:58,017 --> 00:02:59,397
Humans went much further.

52
00:02:59,417 --> 00:03:00,755
We buried our dead.

53
00:03:00,775 --> 00:03:03,566
Some researchers argue
it started very early.

54
00:03:03,586 --> 00:03:09,126
In a deep cave system in South Africa,
they found the remains of Homo naledi, a

55
00:03:09,146 --> 00:03:14,526
small-brained relative of ours, dated to
between about two hundred and thirty six

56
00:03:14,546 --> 00:03:18,709
thousand, and three hundred and
thirty five thousand years ago.

57
00:03:18,729 --> 00:03:22,334
The team thinks some bodies
were placed in dug graves.

58
00:03:22,354 --> 00:03:25,160
But many scientists aren't convinced yet.

59
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:28,603
The first burials almost
everyone agrees on come later.

60
00:03:28,623 --> 00:03:33,915
At Qafzeh Cave in Israel, between about
ninety and a hundred thousand years ago,

61
00:03:33,935 --> 00:03:39,033
a teenager was laid in a grave with
deer antlers held against the chest.

62
00:03:39,053 --> 00:03:43,221
Years before, that teenager had
survived a serious head injury.

63
00:03:43,241 --> 00:03:48,128
In Kenya, in a cave called Panga ya
Saidi, archaeologists found a tiny

64
00:03:48,148 --> 00:03:51,472
skeleton. A child, about three years old.

65
00:03:51,492 --> 00:03:56,464
The researchers named the child
Mtoto, which means child in Swahili.

66
00:03:56,484 --> 00:04:01,352
Seventy eight thousand years ago, someone
laid Mtoto on its side, with the knees

67
00:04:01,372 --> 00:04:04,008
drawn up, probably wrapped in a shroud.

68
00:04:04,028 --> 00:04:08,521
The head seems to have rested on
something soft that later rotted away.

69
00:04:08,541 --> 00:04:13,166
Perhaps a pillow. A pillow, for
a child who would never wake up.

70
00:04:13,186 --> 00:04:15,164
Neanderthals buried their dead too.

71
00:04:15,184 --> 00:04:20,551
At La Chapelle aux Saints in France,
researchers argue that a Neanderthal man was

72
00:04:20,571 --> 00:04:25,099
laid in a pit dug for him,
around fifty thousand years ago.

73
00:04:25,119 --> 00:04:28,474
You may have heard of the
Neanderthal flower burial.

74
00:04:28,494 --> 00:04:33,996
At Shanidar Cave in Iraq, scientists
found ancient pollen around a skeleton.

75
00:04:34,016 --> 00:04:38,085
For decades, people pictured mourners
covering the body with flowers.

76
00:04:38,105 --> 00:04:42,477
But the pollen was most likely carried
in by bees that dig their nests in the

77
00:04:42,497 --> 00:04:47,085
ground. Science sometimes
takes away the beautiful story.

78
00:04:47,105 --> 00:04:52,023
But Neanderthals at Shanidar do seem to
have laid their dead in one particular

79
00:04:52,043 --> 00:04:53,773
place in the cave.

80
00:04:53,793 --> 00:04:56,633
And Neanderthals did
something even stranger.

81
00:04:56,653 --> 00:05:01,458
Deep inside Bruniquel Cave in France,
more than three hundred metres from

82
00:05:01,478 --> 00:05:06,158
daylight, someone broke off around four
hundred stalagmites, and arranged them

83
00:05:06,178 --> 00:05:08,341
into rings and piles.

84
00:05:08,361 --> 00:05:11,791
Every one of the structures
shows traces of fire.

85
00:05:11,811 --> 00:05:15,758
They're about a hundred and
seventy six thousand years old.

86
00:05:15,778 --> 00:05:18,308
Nobody knows what the rings were for.

87
00:05:18,328 --> 00:05:22,746
But someone carried a light into total
darkness, to build something no one would

88
00:05:22,766 --> 00:05:24,887
ever see from outside.

89
00:05:24,907 --> 00:05:28,199
To believe in things you
can't see, you need symbols.

90
00:05:28,219 --> 00:05:30,529
Things that stand for something else.

91
00:05:30,549 --> 00:05:32,179
One of the oldest is a colour.

92
00:05:32,199 --> 00:05:37,386
Red. In Kenya, around three hundred
thousand years ago, people were already

93
00:05:37,406 --> 00:05:41,378
grinding red, iron-rich rock,
probably for its colour.

94
00:05:41,398 --> 00:05:46,951
In Blombos Cave in South Africa, people
scratched crisscross patterns into lumps

95
00:05:46,971 --> 00:05:51,932
of ochre, between about a hundred thousand
and seventy five thousand years ago.

96
00:05:51,952 --> 00:05:56,807
And about seventy three thousand years
ago, someone used an ochre crayon to draw

97
00:05:56,827 --> 00:06:00,660
on a stone flake. It's
the oldest drawing known.

98
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:05,039
The lines run off the edge, so it
was once part of something bigger.

99
00:06:05,059 --> 00:06:07,946
Then the graves begin to
fill with beautiful things.

100
00:06:07,966 --> 00:06:13,350
In Wales, in eighteen twenty three, a
geologist found a skeleton stained red with

101
00:06:13,370 --> 00:06:16,988
ochre. He called it the
Red Lady of Paviland.

102
00:06:17,008 --> 00:06:22,138
But the Red Lady was really a young man,
buried about thirty three thousand years

103
00:06:22,158 --> 00:06:24,480
ago, with ivory ornaments.

104
00:06:24,500 --> 00:06:29,805
At Sungir in Russia, about thirty four
thousand years ago, two boys were buried

105
00:06:29,825 --> 00:06:34,814
head to head. Their clothes were covered
in more than ten thousand tiny beads,

106
00:06:34,834 --> 00:06:36,914
carved from mammoth ivory.

107
00:06:36,934 --> 00:06:40,784
A man buried nearby wore
about three thousand more.

108
00:06:40,804 --> 00:06:45,049
Making all those beads would have
taken thousands of hours of work.

109
00:06:45,069 --> 00:06:49,296
Long spears, made from straightened
mammoth tusks, lay beside the boys.

110
00:06:49,316 --> 00:06:52,859
Why spend all that time on
people who were already gone?

111
00:06:52,879 --> 00:06:57,661
Maybe because, to the people who buried
them, they weren't completely gone.

112
00:06:57,681 --> 00:06:58,888
Then came the art.

113
00:06:58,908 --> 00:07:04,296
On the Indonesian island of Sulawesi,
there's a cave painting at least fifty one

114
00:07:04,316 --> 00:07:05,767
thousand years old.

115
00:07:05,787 --> 00:07:09,692
It shows a wild pig, and three
human-like figures around it.

116
00:07:09,712 --> 00:07:12,301
It's the oldest story scene known.

117
00:07:12,321 --> 00:07:17,130
In another Sulawesi cave, there's a
hunting scene at least forty eight thousand

118
00:07:17,150 --> 00:07:21,822
years old. Some of the hunters seem to
have the heads of animals, or birds.

119
00:07:21,842 --> 00:07:24,536
Beings that are part
human, and part animal.

120
00:07:24,556 --> 00:07:26,249
They don't exist in nature.

121
00:07:26,269 --> 00:07:28,406
Someone had to imagine them.

122
00:07:28,426 --> 00:07:33,582
In a cave in southern Germany, archaeologists
found a figure carved from mammoth

123
00:07:33,602 --> 00:07:37,171
ivory. It was in hundreds
of broken pieces.

124
00:07:37,191 --> 00:07:41,657
Put back together, it's about thirty
one centimetres tall, and about forty

125
00:07:41,677 --> 00:07:43,170
thousand years old.

126
00:07:43,190 --> 00:07:46,481
It has a human body, and
the head of a cave lion.

127
00:07:46,501 --> 00:07:48,009
It's called the Lion Man.

128
00:07:48,029 --> 00:07:52,024
Carving it with stone tools would
have taken hundreds of hours.

129
00:07:52,044 --> 00:07:56,499
Nearby caves held some of the
oldest musical instruments known.

130
00:07:56,519 --> 00:08:01,736
Flutes. One is made from a vulture's wing
bone, with five finger holes, around

131
00:08:01,756 --> 00:08:03,942
forty thousand years old.

132
00:08:03,962 --> 00:08:06,667
Music. Stories. Imagined beings.

133
00:08:06,687 --> 00:08:11,486
By forty thousand years ago, the
human imagination was in full flight.

134
00:08:11,506 --> 00:08:14,454
Half human, half animal
figures keep appearing.

135
00:08:14,474 --> 00:08:19,867
Deep inside Lascaux Cave in France, about
twenty one thousand years ago, someone

136
00:08:19,887 --> 00:08:24,967
painted a stick man with a bird's head,
falling back in front of a wounded bison.

137
00:08:24,987 --> 00:08:27,997
It's the only human
figure in the whole cave.

138
00:08:28,017 --> 00:08:32,697
And in the Trois-Freres cave, high on
a wall above a chamber full of animal

139
00:08:32,717 --> 00:08:36,934
engravings, there's a figure
archaeologists call the Sorcerer.

140
00:08:36,954 --> 00:08:39,229
Part human, part animal.

141
00:08:39,249 --> 00:08:42,385
Some scientists noticed
something else about these caves.

142
00:08:42,405 --> 00:08:47,148
In certain caves, the paintings tend to
cluster where the human voice resonates

143
00:08:47,168 --> 00:08:52,889
most. Other studies found the link much
weaker, so it's still an open question.

144
00:08:52,909 --> 00:08:58,053
Some stalactites even show wear that
suggests they were struck, like stone drums.

145
00:08:58,073 --> 00:08:59,852
Picture it. A flickering lamp.

146
00:08:59,872 --> 00:09:02,539
Painted animals, moving in the shadows.

147
00:09:02,559 --> 00:09:05,582
And voices, echoing back from the dark.

148
00:09:05,602 --> 00:09:06,882
What were these caves for?

149
00:09:06,902 --> 00:09:08,519
We can't ask the painters.

150
00:09:08,539 --> 00:09:12,211
But some researchers look for
clues in living hunter-gatherers.

151
00:09:12,231 --> 00:09:15,373
In the Kalahari, the Ju'hoansi
hold a healing dance.

152
00:09:15,393 --> 00:09:18,798
The women sit around the
fire, clapping and singing.

153
00:09:18,818 --> 00:09:22,537
The men dance around them,
sometimes all night long.

154
00:09:22,557 --> 00:09:24,700
Some dancers enter a trance.

155
00:09:24,720 --> 00:09:30,700
They say a healing energy, called num,
boils up inside them, rising up the spine.

156
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:34,481
Then they lay their hands on
people, to draw out sickness.

157
00:09:34,501 --> 00:09:38,964
Roughly half of all adult men
become healers, and some women too.

158
00:09:38,984 --> 00:09:44,109
The archaeologist David Lewis-Williams
argued that much of the ancient rock art

159
00:09:44,129 --> 00:09:48,285
of southern Africa shows the
visions and feelings of trance.

160
00:09:48,305 --> 00:09:52,021
It's an influential idea,
though not everyone agrees.

161
00:09:52,041 --> 00:09:55,798
Sometimes, archaeologists find
someone who may have been a healer.

162
00:09:55,818 --> 00:10:01,451
In Hilazon Tachtit cave in Israel, about
twelve thousand years ago, a small,

163
00:10:01,471 --> 00:10:04,345
elderly woman was buried with great care.

164
00:10:04,365 --> 00:10:06,879
She had a disability,
and walked with a limp.

165
00:10:06,899 --> 00:10:10,842
Around her lay fifty tortoise
shells, and parts of rare animals.

166
00:10:10,862 --> 00:10:12,442
An eagle. A leopard.

167
00:10:12,462 --> 00:10:16,859
A wild boar. And the people who
buried her held a great feast.

168
00:10:16,879 --> 00:10:21,234
The excavators think she may have been
a shaman, someone believed to travel

169
00:10:21,254 --> 00:10:24,676
between the human world,
and the world of spirits.

170
00:10:24,696 --> 00:10:26,775
So what did hunter-gatherers believe?

171
00:10:26,795 --> 00:10:30,980
In twenty sixteen, researchers
compared the beliefs of thirty three

172
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,037
hunter-gatherer societies
from around the world.

173
00:10:34,057 --> 00:10:36,737
One belief turned up in every single one.

174
00:10:36,757 --> 00:10:42,335
Animism. The idea that animals, plants,
rivers and mountains have spirits.

175
00:10:42,355 --> 00:10:45,374
That the whole world is alive, and aware.

176
00:10:45,394 --> 00:10:49,762
About four in five groups believed
in some kind of life after death.

177
00:10:49,782 --> 00:10:52,276
About four in five had shamans.

178
00:10:52,296 --> 00:10:55,143
Nearly half honoured the
spirits of their ancestors.

179
00:10:55,163 --> 00:10:56,861
But one thing was rare.

180
00:10:56,881 --> 00:11:00,760
A powerful god who takes an
interest in how people behave.

181
00:11:00,780 --> 00:11:03,722
Only about one in seven groups had one.

182
00:11:03,742 --> 00:11:08,687
Gods like that are much more common
in large, complex societies.

183
00:11:08,707 --> 00:11:13,534
When people began to farm and settle
down, the ancestors moved in with them.

184
00:11:13,554 --> 00:11:18,659
At Catalhoyuk in Turkey, about nine thousand
years ago, people buried their dead

185
00:11:18,679 --> 00:11:21,359
beneath the floors of their own houses.

186
00:11:21,379 --> 00:11:25,014
Sometimes right under the
platforms where they slept.

187
00:11:25,034 --> 00:11:29,764
At Jericho, people kept the skulls of
some of the dead, and rebuilt their faces

188
00:11:29,784 --> 00:11:33,904
in plaster. They set seashells
in place of the eyes.

189
00:11:33,924 --> 00:11:35,690
It sounds strange to us.

190
00:11:35,710 --> 00:11:39,308
But it may have been a way
of keeping loved ones close.

191
00:11:39,328 --> 00:11:43,533
For a hunter-gatherer, the sacred
wasn't somewhere you went once a week.

192
00:11:43,553 --> 00:11:45,139
It was all around you.

193
00:11:45,159 --> 00:11:46,777
In the animal you hunted.

194
00:11:46,797 --> 00:11:48,402
In the river you drank from.

195
00:11:48,422 --> 00:11:50,745
In the stars you walked under.

196
00:11:50,765 --> 00:11:55,863
Then, about eleven and a half thousand
years ago, something astonishing happened.

197
00:11:55,883 --> 00:12:01,088
On a hilltop in southern Turkey, people
began raising enormous stone pillars.

198
00:12:01,108 --> 00:12:02,696
This is Gobekli Tepe.

199
00:12:02,716 --> 00:12:07,746
The pillars are shaped like giant T's,
the tallest about five and a half metres.

200
00:12:07,766 --> 00:12:10,184
They weigh up to about ten tonnes.

201
00:12:10,204 --> 00:12:11,835
And they're covered in carvings.

202
00:12:11,855 --> 00:12:13,460
Foxes. Boars. Snakes.

203
00:12:13,480 --> 00:12:19,166
Vultures. Some pillars even have arms
and hands carved along their sides, as if

204
00:12:19,186 --> 00:12:20,815
they were giant people.

205
00:12:20,835 --> 00:12:22,740
And here's the surprising part.

206
00:12:22,760 --> 00:12:25,315
The people who built it
were hunter-gatherers.

207
00:12:25,335 --> 00:12:27,541
Farming had barely begun.

208
00:12:27,561 --> 00:12:32,191
The archaeologist who excavated it,
Klaus Schmidt, called it a sanctuary.

209
00:12:32,211 --> 00:12:35,533
He said, first came the
temple, then the city.

210
00:12:35,553 --> 00:12:40,958
Newer digs have found houses, grinding
stones, and cisterns carved into the rock

211
00:12:40,978 --> 00:12:42,545
to collect rainwater.

212
00:12:42,565 --> 00:12:45,244
So people probably lived there too.

213
00:12:45,264 --> 00:12:47,796
Later monuments were
built to catch the sky.

214
00:12:47,816 --> 00:12:52,659
At Newgrange in Ireland, about five thousand
two hundred years ago, people built

215
00:12:52,679 --> 00:12:55,971
a huge mound with a long stone passage.

216
00:12:55,991 --> 00:13:01,034
Once a year, around the shortest day of
winter, the rising sun shines through a

217
00:13:01,054 --> 00:13:05,471
small opening above the door, and lights
up the chamber at the heart of the mound

218
00:13:05,491 --> 00:13:07,706
for about seventeen minutes.

219
00:13:07,726 --> 00:13:13,364
It's older than Stonehenge's stone circle,
and older than the Pyramids of Giza.

220
00:13:13,384 --> 00:13:17,927
At Stonehenge, the great stones line
up with the midsummer sunrise, and the

221
00:13:17,947 --> 00:13:23,035
midwinter sunset. The smaller bluestones
were brought from Wales, more than two

222
00:13:23,055 --> 00:13:25,098
hundred kilometres away.

223
00:13:25,118 --> 00:13:29,673
And the six tonne Altar Stone came from
the north east of Scotland, at least

224
00:13:29,693 --> 00:13:32,764
seven hundred and fifty kilometres away.

225
00:13:32,784 --> 00:13:37,852
Nearby, archaeologists found the
remains of huge winter feasts.

226
00:13:37,872 --> 00:13:42,564
The pigs were raised all over Britain,
some as far away as Scotland, and brought

227
00:13:42,584 --> 00:13:44,204
there to be eaten.

228
00:13:44,224 --> 00:13:48,953
So why? Why would people who lived on the
edge of survival spend so much time on

229
00:13:48,973 --> 00:13:51,724
rituals? One answer might be this.

230
00:13:51,744 --> 00:13:53,963
Rituals bring people together.

231
00:13:53,983 --> 00:13:58,119
In twenty sixteen, researchers
held a silent disco experiment.

232
00:13:58,139 --> 00:14:02,569
When people danced in sync with each
other, they felt closer to the group.

233
00:14:02,589 --> 00:14:06,819
And they could tolerate more pain
afterwards, a sign that their brains were

234
00:14:06,839 --> 00:14:09,239
releasing feel-good chemicals.

235
00:14:09,259 --> 00:14:14,325
In Mauritius, researchers studied a
very demanding religious festival.

236
00:14:14,345 --> 00:14:19,312
Men who took part in its painful ritual,
or just watched it, later gave more

237
00:14:19,332 --> 00:14:23,155
money to the temple than men
who only joined the prayers.

238
00:14:23,175 --> 00:14:25,405
Dancing together. Singing together.

239
00:14:25,425 --> 00:14:26,692
Feasting together.

240
00:14:26,712 --> 00:14:30,930
Rituals may have helped turn a
group of individuals into a team.

241
00:14:30,950 --> 00:14:33,823
And sometimes, the evidence
is simply about love.

242
00:14:33,843 --> 00:14:39,180
At Ain Mallaha in Israel, about twelve
thousand years ago, an older person was

243
00:14:39,200 --> 00:14:42,402
buried with one hand resting on a puppy.

244
00:14:42,422 --> 00:14:47,752
At Oberkassel in Germany, about fourteen
thousand years ago, a man and a woman

245
00:14:47,772 --> 00:14:49,516
were buried with a dog.

246
00:14:49,536 --> 00:14:52,566
The dog was a puppy,
about seven months old.

247
00:14:52,586 --> 00:14:56,229
And its teeth show it had been
very sick, again and again.

248
00:14:56,249 --> 00:15:00,137
It probably only survived for
weeks because people nursed it.

249
00:15:00,157 --> 00:15:01,862
Someone cared for that puppy.

250
00:15:01,882 --> 00:15:04,943
And when it died, they
buried it beside them.

251
00:15:04,963 --> 00:15:06,905
Maybe that's where it all begins.

252
00:15:06,925 --> 00:15:09,018
Not with gods, or temples.

253
00:15:09,038 --> 00:15:13,268
But with love, and loss, and the
feeling that the ones we lose don't just

254
00:15:13,288 --> 00:15:14,595
disappear.

255
00:15:14,615 --> 00:15:17,967
Today, we build churches,
mosques and temples.

256
00:15:17,987 --> 00:15:21,308
We have holy books, and
calendars full of festivals.

257
00:15:21,328 --> 00:15:23,924
But the oldest rituals are still with us.

258
00:15:23,944 --> 00:15:26,149
We still gather when someone dies.

259
00:15:26,169 --> 00:15:29,324
We still light fires
and candles in the dark.

260
00:15:29,344 --> 00:15:32,454
And we still look up at
the stars, and wonder.

261
00:15:32,474 --> 00:15:36,253
Fifty thousand years ago, a
small group stood around a grave.

262
00:15:36,273 --> 00:15:37,516
They had no temple.

263
00:15:37,536 --> 00:15:42,903
No holy book. But they had red ochre,
and beads, and stories by the fire.

264
00:15:42,923 --> 00:15:48,101
And a world full of spirits, in every
animal, every river, and every star.

265
00:15:48,121 --> 00:15:49,201
You visit a temple.

266
00:15:49,221 --> 00:15:51,296
They lived inside one.
